- Missing `init` declaration: No `init(...)` node is declared despite grammar requirements, preventing a valid process start even though the source states "To start the SSP process..." (SMC1, SMC5).
- `branch(b1, XOR)`, `precedence(b1, n9)`, `branch(b2, XOR)`, `precedence(b2, n12)`: The model lacks backward precedence edges to form cycles, failing to capture that processing should be "repeated until all invoices are processed" and clearing is "done every hour until the outbox is completely cleared" (SMC1, SMC4).
- `defines(v1, "More batches to process?", {"No"})` and `defines(v2, "Outbox completely cleared?", {"Yes"})`: Variable ranges are restricted to single values, making it impossible to evaluate the positive/negative decision outcomes required for loop continuation implied by the source's "until..." conditions (SMC6).
- `actor(a_default, "Default", [n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6, n7, n8, b1, n9, n10, n11, b2, n12, e1])`: All nodes are assigned to a generic participant, contradicting the source which explicitly designates the "Data Entry Officer" to receive allocated invoices and own the outbox being cleared (SMC7).
- `label(n1, n2, "Yes")`, `label(n2, n3, "Yes")`, `label(n3, n4, "Yes")`, `label(n4, n5, "Yes")`, `label(n5, n6, "Yes")`, `label(n9, n10, "Yes")`: These labels hallucinate explicit conditional gates on sequential edges, whereas the source describes a straightforward chronological progression ("After that...", "Once... confirmed...", "Once... checked...") without requiring labeled decision points (SMC1, SMC2).

Structural Check
- [S1] STC8 failed: Each action node must have exactly one incoming and one outgoing edge. | Node `n1` has in=0 and out=1. | DSL: action(n1,"start")
- [S2] STC9 failed: Each branch node must have exactly one incoming and at least two outgoing edges. | Node `b1` has in=1 and out=1. | Node `b2` has in=1 and out=1. | DSL: branch(b1,XOR); branch(b2,XOR)
- [S3] STC11 failed: Each variable definition must have a valid range with at least two distinct values. | Variable `v1` has an invalid range `['No']`. | Variable `v2` has an invalid range `['Yes']`.
- [S4] STC15 failed: Every variable used in a guard must be formally defined. | Guard `v1 = 'No'` on `b1 -> n9` uses undefined variable `v1`. | Guard `v2 = 'Yes'` on `b2 -> n12` uses undefined variable `v2`. | DSL: guard(b1,n9,"v1 = 'No'"); guard(b2,n12,"v2 = 'Yes'")
- [S5] STC19 failed: Every non-init node must be reachable from the init node. | This reachability check requires exactly one init node. | Violations: b1, b2, e1, n1, n10, n11, n12, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6, n7, n8, n9 | DSL: branch(b1,XOR); branch(b2,XOR); end(e1); action(n1,"start"); action(n10,"Clear outbox every hour until completely cleared"); action(n11,"Outbox completely cleared?"); action(n12,"End of process"); action(n2,"Ensure invoice is ready for verification") (+7 more)

Logical Sanity Check
- [L1] LSC1 failed: Every guard must be satisfiable under the declared ranges of its referenced variables. | Guard `v1 = 'No'` on `b1 -> n9` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Guard `v2 = 'Yes'` on `b2 -> n12` cannot be evaluated: `v2` has no declared range. | DSL: guard(b1,n9,"v1 = 'No'"); guard(b2,n12,"v2 = 'Yes'")
- [L2] LSC3 failed: Guards outgoing from XOR/OR branches must cover the whole evaluated state space. | Branch `b1` cannot be evaluated: `v1` has no declared range. | Branch `b2` cannot be evaluated: `v2` has no declared range.
